From a548a5ce610f5669f5c161f71652da08e91fd939 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 07:14:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] Document rate limiting as per-client, not per-IP

The limiter is keyed on the authenticated client id
(_check_rate_limit(client["client_id"])), and authentication runs
before the rate-limit check, so unauthenticated requests were never
throttled. The README claimed per-IP limiting in two places; describe
the actual behavior and its implications instead.
---
 README.md | 5 +++--
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 689ffad..5a2eaca 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -13,7 +13,7 @@ A minimal, single-file Python tool for ephemeral file sharing on local networks.
 - **Presentation mode**: Control what all clients see from the CLI
 - **Shared pad**: Real-time shared text pad between all clients
 - **CLI client**: Upload files, list files, and control previews from command line
-- **Rate limiting**: Per-IP sliding window rate limiting on write endpoints
+- **Rate limiting**: Per-client sliding window rate limiting on write endpoints
 - **Storage limits**: Per-client storage caps (4GB default)
 - **Tested**: Comprehensive E2E test suite included
 
@@ -175,7 +175,8 @@ The build process:
 
 - Files are stored in memory without encryption (E2E encryption planned for future)
 - Per-client Bearer token authentication with salted SHA-256 hashing (timing-attack safe)
-- Per-IP rate limiting on write endpoints (30 requests/60s sliding window)
+- Per-client rate limiting on write endpoints (30 requests/60s sliding window, keyed by client id — all devices sharing a client id share one window)
+- Unauthenticated requests are not rate limited: rejecting them costs at most one hash, and credential brute force is impractical against the 64-hex-char generated secrets (pick long secrets if you provide your own)
 - Per-client storage caps (4GB default)
 - No HTTPS support (use a reverse proxy if needed)
 - Listens dual-stack on `[::]:<port>` — reachable from both IPv4 and IPv6 clients on the LAN (falls back to IPv4-only on hosts without IPv6)
-- 
